.Dd February 20, 2026
.Dt ZSTREAM 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zstream
.Nd manipulate ZFS send streams
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl t Ar token
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Cm command Op Ar args Ns ...
.Nm
.Cm dump
.Op Fl Cvd
.Op Ar file
.Nm
.Cm decompress
.Op Fl v
.Op Ar object Ns Sy \&, Ns Ar offset Ns Op Sy \&, Ns Ar type Ns ...
.Nm
.Cm drop_record
.Op Fl v
.Op Ar object Ns Sy \&, Ns Ar offset Ns Op Sy \& Ns ...
.Nm
.Cm redup
.Op Fl v
.Ar file
.Nm
.Cm token
.Ar resume_token
.Nm
.Cm token
.Fl g
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Nm
.Cm recompress
.Op Fl l Ar level
.Ar algorithm
.Nm
.Cm resume
.Fl t Ar token
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Op Fl c Ar size
.Op Fl S
.Op Fl H Ar header_file
.Nm
.Cm split
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Fl c Ar size
.Op Fl o Ar prefix
.Nm
.Cm join
.Op Fl i Ar head
.Ar partial Ns ...
.
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Sy zstream
utility manipulates ZFS send streams output by the
.Sy zfs send
command.
.Ss Global flags
The following flags may be specified
.Em before
the subcommand name to control input setup for all subcommands:
.Bl -tag -width "-t"
.It Fl t Ar token
Fast-rewind: decode the resume
.Ar token
to obtain the stream byte offset
.Pq if present in the token ,
seek the input file to that point, and validate the position with a probe read.
This eliminates the linear scan normally required to reach the resume point,
making resume O(1) on stream size.
Requires
.Fl i Ar file
with seekable input.
.It Fl i Ar file
Input file.
Required when
.Fl t
is used; optional otherwise (subcommands default to stdin).
.El
.Bl -tag -width ""
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm dump
.Op Fl Cvd
.Op Ar file
.Xc
Print information about the specified send stream, including headers and
record counts.
The send stream may either be in the specified
.Ar file ,
or provided on standard input.
.Bl -tag -width "-D"
.It Fl C
Suppress the validation of checksums.
.It Fl v
Verbose.
Print metadata for each record.
.It Fl d
Dump data contained in each record.
Implies verbose.
.El
.Pp
The
.Nm zstreamdump
alias is provided for compatibility and is equivalent to running
.Nm
.Cm dump .
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm token
.Ar resume_token
.Xc
Dumps ZFS resume token information, decoding the nvlist fields
.Pq object, offset, toguid, etc.\&
from the base64-encoded token string.
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm token
.Fl g
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Xc
Generate a resume token from a
.Pq possibly truncated
send stream.
Reads the stream from
.Ar file
or stdin, scans to the last complete record, and outputs a resume token
on stdout that can later be used with
.Nm zfs Cm send Fl t Ar token
or
.Nm zstream Cm resume Fl t Ar token .
The input need not contain a
.Sy DRR_END
record; a truncated stream at any record boundary is valid.
.Bl -tag -width "-i"
.It Fl g
Generate mode: produce a resume token from the input stream.
.It Fl i Ar file
Input stream file (default: stdin).
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm decompress
.Op Fl v
.Op Ar object Ns Sy \&, Ns Ar offset Ns Op Sy \&, Ns Ar type Ns ...
.Xc
Decompress selected records in a ZFS send stream provided on standard input,
when the compression type recorded in ZFS metadata may be incorrect.
Specify the object number and byte offset of each record that you wish to
decompress.
Optionally specify the compression type.
Valid compression types include
.Sy off ,
.Sy gzip ,
.Sy lz4 ,
.Sy lzjb ,
.Sy zstd ,
and
.Sy zle .
The default is
.Sy lz4 .
Every record for that object beginning at that offset will be decompressed, if
possible.
It may not be possible, because the record may be corrupted in some but not
all of the stream's snapshots.
Specifying a compression type of
.Sy off
will change the stream's metadata accordingly, without attempting decompression.
This can be useful if the record is already uncompressed but the metadata
insists otherwise.
The repaired stream will be written to standard output.
.Bl -tag -width "-v"
.It Fl v
Verbose.
Print summary of decompressed records.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm drop_record
.Op Fl v
.Op Ar object Ns Sy \&, Ns Ar offset Ns Op Sy \& ...
.Xc
Drop selected records from a ZFS send stream provided on standard input,
specified by object number and byte offset.
Only WRITE and WRITE_EMBEDDED are records are supported, currently.
The repaired stream will be written to standard output.
.Bl -tag -width "-v"
.It Fl v
Verbose.
Print summary of dropped records.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm redup
.Op Fl v
.Ar file
.Xc
Deduplicated send streams can be generated by using the
.Nm zfs Cm send Fl D
command.
The ability to send deduplicated send streams is deprecated.
In the future, the ability to receive a deduplicated send stream with
.Nm zfs Cm receive
will be removed.
However, deduplicated send streams can still be received by utilizing
.Nm zstream Cm redup .
.Pp
The
.Nm zstream Cm redup
command is provided a
.Ar file
containing a deduplicated send stream, and outputs an equivalent
non-deduplicated send stream on standard output.
Therefore, a deduplicated send stream can be received by running:
.Dl # Nm zstream Cm redup Pa DEDUP_STREAM_FILE | Nm zfs Cm receive No …
.Bl -tag -width "-D"
.It Fl v
Verbose.
Print summary of converted records.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm recompress
.Op Fl l Ar level
.Ar algorithm
.Xc
Recompresses a send stream, provided on standard input, using the provided
algorithm and optional level, and writes the modified stream to standard output.
All WRITE records in the send stream will be recompressed, unless they fail
to result in size reduction compared to being left uncompressed.
The provided algorithm can be any valid value to the
.Nm compress
property.
Note that encrypted send streams cannot be recompressed.
.Bl -tag -width "-l"
.It Fl l Ar level
Specifies compression level.
Only needed for algorithms where the level is not implied as part of the name
of the algorithm (e.g. gzip-3 does not require it, while zstd does, if a
non-default level is desired).
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm resume
.Fl t Ar token
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Op Fl c Ar size
.Op Fl S
.Op Fl H Ar header_file
.Xc
Read a cached send stream from
.Ar file
or stdin, and produce on stdout a resume stream starting at the point
specified by the resume
.Ar token .
The output can be piped directly to
.Nm zfs Cm receive Fl s .
.Pp
The resume token is typically obtained from a failed receive
.Pq Nm zfs Cm receive Fl s
or generated from a truncated stream with
.Nm zstream Cm token Fl g .
.Bl -tag -width "-o"
.It Fl t Ar token
Resume token (required).
Also accepted as the global
.Fl t
flag before the subcommand name, which additionally seeks the input file.
.It Fl i Ar file
Input cached send stream file (default: stdin).
.It Fl o Ar offset
Explicit stream byte offset in hex.
Overrides the
.Sy stream_offset
field in the resume token.
Used when the token lacks the
.Sy stream_offset
key or a manual override is desired.
The offset is validated with a probe read before streaming begins.
.It Fl c Ar size
Cut stream after
.Ar size
bytes.
Write at most
.Ar size
bytes of resume stream, then stop without writing a
.Sy DRR_END
record.
Useful when producing partial resume chunks to be reassembled with
.Nm zstream Cm join .
.It Fl S
Skip-seek mode.
Do not seek within the input file and do not filter records by
resume point.
All records after
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
are emitted verbatim with recomputed checksums.
The caller is responsible for pre-positioning stdin at the resume point
.Pq e.g., using Xr tail 1 Fl c .
Must be used together with
.Fl H
to provide the original
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
header, since a pre-positioned stream lacks it.
.It Fl H Ar header
File containing the original
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
record and its nvlist payload
.Pq e.g., the first chunk of a split stream ,
or a data URI of the form
.Sy data: Ns Oo Ar type Oc Ns Sy \&;base64, Ns Ar data .
Used with
.Fl S
when the piped input has been pre-positioned past the
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
record.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm split
.Op Fl i Ar file
.Fl c Ar size
.Op Fl o Ar prefix
.Xc
Split a full send stream into fixed-size, record-aligned chunks
that can be reassembled with
.Nm zstream Cm join .
Each chunk begins with a
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
record so it is a self-describing stream fragment.
Chunks are written to files named
.Ar prefix Ns Sy \&. Ns Ar NNN
.Pq zero-padded to 3 digits .
.Pp
The input stream may be a regular file or a pipe (stdin).
On pipe input, a record that would exceed the chunk size is buffered
in memory and written as the first record of the next chunk.
This avoids the need for seeking.
.Bl -tag -width "-o"
.It Fl i Ar file
Input stream file (default: stdin).
.It Fl c Ar size
Maximum uncompressed bytes per chunk (required).
Chunks are aligned to record boundaries, so actual chunk sizes may be
slightly smaller.
The chunk size must be at least 512 and should be large enough to
accommodate the largest record in the stream.
.It Fl o Ar prefix
Output file prefix (default:
.Sy chunk ) .
.El
.It Xo
.Nm
.Cm join
.Op Fl i Ar head
.Ar partial Ns ...
.Xc
Join a send stream head fragment with one or more partial resume
streams into a single output stream.
The head fragment contains the
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
record and the initial data up to the resume point.
The partial fragments are produced by
.Nm zstream Cm resume Fl c Ar size
and are concatenated in order.
.Pp
Exit codes:
.Bl -tag -width "0" -compact
.It 0
Stream is complete
.Pq Sy DRR_END No written .
.It 1
Error.
.It 2
Stream is incomplete
.Pq no Sy DRR_END .
.El
.Bl -tag -width "-i"
.It Fl i Ar head
Head fragment file.
If omitted, the first positional argument is used as the head.
If no positional arguments are given, stdin is used as the head.
.El
.Ar partial
is one or more partial stream files to append after the head.
Each partial must begin with a valid
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
record; these are skipped
.Pq they duplicate the head's DRR_BEGIN ,
and only the data records are copied.
.El
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Recovering from OpenZFS bug #12762
First, determine which records are corrupt.
That cannot be done automatically; it requires information beyond ZFS's
metadata.
If object
.Sy 128
is corrupted at offset
.Sy 0
and is compressed using
.Sy lz4 ,
then run this command:
.Bd -literal
.No # Nm zfs Ar send Fl c Ar … | Nm zstream decompress Ar 128,0,lz4 | \
Nm zfs recv Ar …
.Ed
.
.Ss Recovering from OpenZFS bug #18239
The bogus records typically have an absurdly large offset, and can be seen with
a command like
.Nm zdb Fl ddddd Ar dataset Ar object
or
.Nm zstream Ar dump Fl v .
To recover, send the dataset and use
.Nm zstream
to drop the bogus record, then receive into a new dataset.
.Bd -literal
.No # Nm zfs Ar send Ar ... | Nm zstream drop_record Ar 3545761,18446744073709486080 | \
Nm zfs recv Ar ...
.Ed
.
.Ss Piped resume from pre-positioned stream
When sending a very large snapshot stream, it is often split into chunks
.Pq Nm zstream Cm split
for transport.
To resume from a specific chunk without seeking:
.Bd -literal
.No # Nm zstd Fl d Ar chunk_*.zfs | Nm tail Fl c Sy + Ns Va STREAM_OFFSET | \
    Nm zstream Cm resume Fl t Ar TOKEN Fl H Ar chunk_0000.zfs Fl S | \
    Nm zfs Cm recv Fl s Ar pool/fs
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Va STREAM_OFFSET
value is obtained from the resume token
.Pq Nm zstream Cm token Ar TOKEN ,
and
.Ar chunk_0000.zfs
contains the original
.Sy DRR_BEGIN
record.
.Fl S
disables seeking and resume-point filtering since the input is already positioned.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zdb 8 ,
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-receive 8 ,
.Xr zfs-send 8 ,
.Lk https://github.com/openzfs/zfs/issues/12762
.Lk https://github.com/openzfs/zfs/issues/18239
